00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:57.520
It's time for them to start
looking into what else

00:01:57.520 --> 00:01:58.970
they can do.

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:01.150
And so one of their
developers, Tess,

00:02:01.150 --> 00:02:05.238
is going to lead us into
those new approaches.

00:02:05.238 --> 00:02:07.530
MARTIN OMANDER: It's a shame
they're totally imaginary.

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:09.780
I really like that backpack.

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:12.230
I'd like to buy that.

00:02:12.230 --> 00:02:15.740
So Tess, the developer,
she has this thought about,

00:02:15.740 --> 00:02:19.970
microservices could help us
with release velocity and dev

00:02:19.970 --> 00:02:21.420
velocity.

00:02:21.420 --> 00:02:23.660
Now, she needs to
study up first.

00:02:23.660 --> 00:02:25.520
What are these microservices?

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:27.940
So let's go through
some quick definitions.

00:02:27.940 --> 00:02:31.910
Monolith, that's what most
of your applications--

00:02:31.910 --> 00:02:33.050
you raised your hand--

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:36.740
that's when you have
a big ball of yarn

00:02:36.740 --> 00:02:38.360
and everything's
sort of connected

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.870
or could be connected
to anything else.

00:02:41.870 --> 00:02:46.030
Microservices are when you
have specific services that

00:02:46.030 --> 00:02:50.930
are independently built,
deployed, operated, and scaled.

00:02:50.930 --> 00:02:53.360
Microservices could
take requests.

00:02:53.360 --> 00:02:55.520
They could potentially
call each other, as well,

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:58.370
to fulfill requests.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:01.430
Let's zoom in on one
of those microservices.

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:04.550
What's inside a microservice?

00:03:04.550 --> 00:03:08.280
There are four things that
we think are very important.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.260
There are lots of
definitions out there.

00:03:10.260 --> 00:03:12.260
Adam and I think that
there are four things that

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:13.820
set a microservice apart.

00:03:13.820 --> 00:03:15.557
One is it has an API.

00:03:15.557 --> 00:03:17.390
If you're going to talk
to the microservice,

00:03:17.390 --> 00:03:18.770
you talk to the API.

00:03:18.770 --> 00:03:20.810
No other way in.

00:03:20.810 --> 00:03:22.850
It has some compute resources.

00:03:22.850 --> 00:03:27.260
So this is a way to actually
run code, run business logic.

00:03:27.260 --> 00:03:29.150
Most microservices,
virtually all,

00:03:29.150 --> 00:03:30.980
will have some kind of storage--

00:03:30.980 --> 00:03:36.480
database, file system, in-memory
cache, something like that.

00:03:36.480 --> 00:03:41.240
And then the fourth thing is
actually a non-technical thing.

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:43.700
We think it's very important
that the microservice has

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:46.530
a team associated
with it as well.

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:50.540
So if this service breaks, then
we know what team to talk to.

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:52.640
If this service needs
to be refactored,

00:03:52.640 --> 00:03:55.430
then this team has
full ownership,

00:03:55.430 --> 00:03:58.700
and nobody else can come in and
override what they are doing.

00:03:58.700 --> 00:04:03.290
These guys are the experts
on this microservice.

00:04:03.290 --> 00:04:06.380
So we're going to follow Tess's
microservice journey here.

00:04:06.380 --> 00:04:08.520
She's going to lay
the foundation that

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:11.425
will build some brand
new functionality

00:04:11.425 --> 00:04:13.700
as a microservice.

00:04:13.700 --> 00:04:18.589
After that, Tess and team will
take existing functionality

00:04:18.589 --> 00:04:23.600
in from the monolith and break
that out into a microservice.

00:04:23.600 --> 00:04:25.880
Tess will sit down
and plan with her boss

00:04:25.880 --> 00:04:28.510
for the future of what
to do with microservice

00:04:28.510 --> 00:04:29.910
in the future.

00:04:29.910 --> 00:04:33.620
And finally, there'll be
some contemplating about what

00:04:33.620 --> 00:04:36.920
we have learned here today.

00:04:36.920 --> 00:04:40.090
So first, laying the foundation.

00:04:40.090 --> 00:04:42.602
Doing this sort of digital
transformation, where

00:04:42.602 --> 00:04:44.560
you break up a monolith,
you change everything,

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:47.290
the way all developers are
working at your company,

00:04:47.290 --> 00:04:52.360
also change the way the ops
teams are working requires--

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:53.500
that's not a big bang.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:56.770
That is like 100 small
brown bag lunches,

00:04:56.770 --> 00:05:00.070
a lot of explaining to people
who don't know what these are,

00:05:00.070 --> 00:05:02.830
getting the team onboard.

00:05:02.830 --> 00:05:07.660
After that, Tess would go and
talk to her manager about,

00:05:07.660 --> 00:05:09.160
I want to go the
microservice route.

00:05:09.160 --> 00:05:12.880
The team thinks we should
go the microservice route.

00:05:12.880 --> 00:05:17.080
Her boss is Sang, who is
the CTO of Into The Woods.

00:05:17.080 --> 00:05:21.310
Now, this conversation can
go one of several ways.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:24.280
Tess could go to
Sang and say, we

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:29.360
should break up our monolith,
microservices are great.

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:30.550
What would Sang say?

00:05:30.550 --> 00:05:32.185
Do we have any CTOs
in the audience?

00:05:32.185 --> 00:05:32.815
AUDIENCE: Yeah.

00:05:32.815 --> 00:05:33.490
MARTIN OMANDER: Oh, yeah?

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:33.990
OK.

00:05:33.990 --> 00:05:35.410
Very good.

00:05:35.410 --> 00:05:39.190
I think you would say, CTO there
in the back, yeah, of course,

00:05:39.190 --> 00:05:39.800
let's do it.

00:05:39.800 --> 00:05:41.560
But what's the ROI?

00:05:41.560 --> 00:05:43.540
Is that right?

00:05:43.540 --> 00:05:45.210
OK, very good.

00:05:45.210 --> 00:05:48.010
And this is a hard
conversation, because we

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:49.600
don't have hard numbers.

00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:51.670
As developers, we all
feel that microservices

00:05:51.670 --> 00:05:53.050
are easier to manage and build.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:56.980
But it's really hard to
prove that's the case.

00:05:56.980 --> 00:06:00.010
So Tess bides her time.

00:06:00.010 --> 00:06:03.430
A few weeks later,
Sang comes to Tess

00:06:03.430 --> 00:06:06.400
and says, hey, the
business people,

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:09.470
they want a
recommendation engine.

00:06:09.470 --> 00:06:11.710
So based on what's
in the cart, we

00:06:11.710 --> 00:06:14.770
should show
recommendations to users.

00:06:14.770 --> 00:06:17.260
Now Tess is in a much
better place, because she

00:06:17.260 --> 00:06:19.300
can say, huh, me
and the team, we

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.910
have this great
new approach that

00:06:21.910 --> 00:06:26.590
will give us greater dev
velocity for this new feature

00:06:26.590 --> 00:06:27.940
you want to build.

00:06:27.940 --> 00:06:30.440
And now, Sang is interested.

00:06:30.440 --> 00:06:32.788
So [? Beth ?] tells him more.

00:06:32.788 --> 00:06:34.330
ADAM ROSS: Yeah,
it's great that Tess

00:06:34.330 --> 00:06:37.390
has this opportunity with a new
feature that might be valuable

00:06:37.390 --> 00:06:39.640
but isn't mission
critical immediately.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:42.400
Because it could be faster
for her to build this as part

00:06:42.400 --> 00:06:43.730
of their existing application.

00:06:43.730 --> 00:06:46.658
But as a new service, it'll
take a little more time

00:06:46.658 --> 00:06:47.200
to get right.

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.490
But in the long term, maybe
it'll be more sustainable.

00:06:50.490 --> 00:06:52.690
Tess's approach is
going to be iterative,

00:06:52.690 --> 00:06:54.760
using a lean approach
of making sure

00:06:54.760 --> 00:06:57.250
that each step of the
way through this they've

00:06:57.250 --> 00:07:00.250
made the right decisions and
are building the right thing.

00:07:00.250 --> 00:07:03.760
Initially, the main concern is,
will this particular approach

00:07:03.760 --> 00:07:06.640
of building recommendations
as a microservice

00:07:06.640 --> 00:07:09.100
work on a technical level?

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:13.210
And furthermore, will it work as
a way of having multiple teams

00:07:13.210 --> 00:07:13.870
collaborate?

00:07:13.870 --> 00:07:17.887
Can the API contract prevent
having a lot more meetings

00:07:17.887 --> 00:07:20.095
as a result of having two
different software systems?

00:07:20.095 --> 00:07:22.180
MARTIN OMANDER: Ah, we
don't like meetings, do we?

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:22.620
No.

00:07:22.620 --> 00:07:22.900
ADAM ROSS: No.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:25.450
And if that works out, it's
time to move forward and start

00:07:25.450 --> 00:07:31.690
to see, can this recommendation
system drive some money?

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:36.020
So Tess is using a serverless
approach in her exploration

00:07:36.020 --> 00:07:36.520
here.

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:37.895
And as we discussed
earlier, that

00:07:37.895 --> 00:07:40.930
has a number of advantages,
especially for an R&amp;D effort

00:07:40.930 --> 00:07:44.500
like this, where she doesn't
have the backing of a big ops

00:07:44.500 --> 00:07:47.890
team and doesn't have a
large pre-established budget.

00:07:47.890 --> 00:07:51.310
Serverless allows a low
infrastructural management

00:07:51.310 --> 00:07:53.420
solution and a
pay-as-you-go model,

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:56.020
which means the experiments they
run are the experiments they

00:07:56.020 --> 00:07:56.530
pay for.

00:07:59.530 --> 00:08:02.980
There are three main
serverless options on GCP--

00:08:02.980 --> 00:08:05.110
Cloud Functions, App
Engine, and, as you

00:08:05.110 --> 00:08:07.660
may have heard this
morning, Cloud Run,

00:08:07.660 --> 00:08:11.020
a new product that allows
serverless containers

00:08:11.020 --> 00:08:12.370
to be run.

00:08:12.370 --> 00:08:14.990
For now though, Cloud Functions
seems like the way to go.

00:08:14.990 --> 00:08:18.070
This is a very highly scoped
recommendation service.

00:08:18.070 --> 00:08:22.430
And that fits with the
tight focus of a function.

00:08:22.430 --> 00:08:24.730
Here's the architecture of
the recommendation service.

00:08:24.730 --> 00:08:29.590
The monolith is going to make
requests of the recommendation

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:32.182
service, sending the product
IDs from the shopping cart.

00:08:32.182 --> 00:08:33.640
And the recommendation
service will

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:37.539
send the recommended
product IDs back.

00:08:37.539 --> 00:08:40.809
For now, this is going to
be a very simple system.

00:08:40.809 --> 00:08:42.767
Dave from marketing
has a couple ideas

00:08:42.767 --> 00:08:44.600
of what they should be
recommending that day

00:08:44.600 --> 00:08:46.100
and is going to
tell the development

00:08:46.100 --> 00:08:49.236
team to go ahead and deploy
those recommendations.

00:08:49.236 --> 00:08:51.930
MARTIN OMANDER: So these are
hard-coded recommendations

00:08:51.930 --> 00:08:52.580
more or less?

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:53.272
ADAM ROSS: Yes.

00:08:53.272 --> 00:08:55.480
But the important thing is
that those recommendations

00:08:55.480 --> 00:08:57.700
eventually show up on
a shopping cart page.

00:08:57.700 --> 00:08:59.890
MARTIN OMANDER: Hm.

00:08:59.890 --> 00:09:05.260
ADAM ROSS: So there's always a
few challenges from that CTO,

00:09:05.260 --> 00:09:07.495
or from any kind of
technical review.

00:09:07.495 --> 00:09:08.870
Can you really
proceed with this?

00:09:08.870 --> 00:09:09.578
Will it work out?

00:09:09.578 --> 00:09:11.340
What if that new
service doesn't respond?

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.670
Well, as a slightly
lower priority service,

00:09:13.670 --> 00:09:16.970
asynchronous requests from
the monolith with a time-out

00:09:16.970 --> 00:09:19.910
will allow it to move on if
that service has an interruption

00:09:19.910 --> 00:09:22.850
and does not respond
with a recommendation.

00:09:22.850 --> 00:09:24.770
Is there a scaling challenge?

00:09:24.770 --> 00:09:26.780
Well, a serverless
approach allows

00:09:26.780 --> 00:09:29.840
you to scale up horizontally
quite quickly and then

00:09:29.840 --> 00:09:32.240
back down again to zero
when you're no longer making

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:34.980
recommendation requests.

00:09:34.980 --> 00:09:38.360
And for setup and maintenance,
well, Google engineering

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.710
is helping you with that
infrastructure challenge.

00:09:40.710 --> 00:09:43.200
So you don't have to carry
a pager for the networking

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:43.700
issues.

00:09:46.510 --> 00:09:49.500
So we're back to the
code shown earlier.

00:09:49.500 --> 00:09:52.770
This is the first iteration
of a recommendation service.

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:55.530
And it is deployed and
working successfully.

00:09:58.440 --> 00:10:00.830
So with that as a success,
it's time to move on

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:02.510
to the next iteration.

00:10:02.510 --> 00:10:05.210
Can they actually drive
some business value

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:08.540
by iterating this service
forward and making

00:10:08.540 --> 00:10:11.390
smarter and more
intelligent recommendations?

00:10:11.390 --> 00:10:13.580
If that works, make
it even better.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:15.590
And if not, maybe
their algorithm

00:10:15.590 --> 00:10:19.050
isn't working out so well,
only recommending socks all day

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:19.550
long.

00:10:19.550 --> 00:10:21.217
MARTIN OMANDER: Well,
you do need socks,

00:10:21.217 --> 00:10:23.487
lots of socks for camping.

00:10:23.487 --> 00:10:25.070
ADAM ROSS: So the
architecture that we

00:10:25.070 --> 00:10:29.687
have for this second iteration
might look a little like this.

00:10:29.687 --> 00:10:30.770
This is probably familiar.

00:10:30.770 --> 00:10:33.930
It's the same API as we
saw on the earlier slide.

00:10:33.930 --> 00:10:35.990
But because the
recommendation service

00:10:35.990 --> 00:10:38.180
is separated from
the monolith, it

00:10:38.180 --> 00:10:39.860
can change its
implementation and get

00:10:39.860 --> 00:10:43.690
more intelligent without the
monolith being any the wiser.

00:10:43.690 --> 00:10:47.020
Now, instead of Tess
deploying changes

00:10:47.020 --> 00:10:49.180
when Dave from marketing
makes requests,

00:10:49.180 --> 00:10:51.940
Dave has access to a Google
Sheet, into which she

00:10:51.940 --> 00:10:55.930
can enter the
recommendations, using it

00:10:55.930 --> 00:10:59.650
as a quick and dirty back office
application, instead of Tess

00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:02.680
and team needing to write a
whole additional application

00:11:02.680 --> 00:11:04.502
and database to pull that off.

00:11:04.502 --> 00:11:06.460
Martin, could you show
us what that looks like?

00:11:06.460 --> 00:11:07.335
MARTIN OMANDER: Yeah.

00:11:07.335 --> 00:11:10.960
Switch to the demo, please.

00:11:10.960 --> 00:11:13.810
So I am now wearing
the marketing hat.

00:11:13.810 --> 00:11:15.790
I'm Dave in marketing.

00:11:15.790 --> 00:11:19.900
Up here, we have the URL
for the microservice.

00:11:19.900 --> 00:11:24.610
The only product in the
cart right now is a map.

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:27.070
So if we hit the
microservice, it

00:11:27.070 --> 00:11:30.260
returns that if you
have a map in your cart,

00:11:30.260 --> 00:11:32.530
a compass is recommended.

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:35.060
That is because
Dave has entered,

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:37.210
if the requested
product is a map,

00:11:37.210 --> 00:11:40.000
the recommended
product is a compass.

00:11:40.000 --> 00:11:44.730
And if you have
more products here,

00:11:44.730 --> 00:11:47.400
so one product was the map.

00:11:47.400 --> 00:11:51.300
Another one could be boots.

00:11:51.300 --> 00:11:54.090
And then we get the
compass and laces.

00:11:54.090 --> 00:11:56.820
So boots leads to laces.

00:11:56.820 --> 00:12:00.940
Now, Dave loves this, because
he can go in here and say,

00:12:00.940 --> 00:12:05.130
well, actually, we are
rebranding our store.

00:12:05.130 --> 00:12:06.630
Wouldn't it be
cool, Dave thinks.

00:12:06.630 --> 00:12:10.170
We should be a
nautical camping store.

00:12:10.170 --> 00:12:15.810
Let's send a sextant to
people as a recommendation,

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:20.370
if they have bought a map or if
they have a map in their cart.

00:12:20.370 --> 00:12:23.760
So now, when we reload,
now we see that a sextant

00:12:23.760 --> 00:12:25.590
is recommended here.

00:12:25.590 --> 00:12:28.560
This gives marketing
great power.

00:12:28.560 --> 00:12:32.570
They love how they can edit
these as many times a day

00:12:32.570 --> 00:12:35.040
as they feel like.

00:12:35.040 --> 00:12:37.080
Let's have a look at
the code for this.

00:12:37.080 --> 00:12:40.860
Switch back to the
slides, please.

00:12:40.860 --> 00:12:47.730
So the code to read from a
Google Sheet is quite easy.

00:12:47.730 --> 00:12:53.740
First, we create an auth object
here with a spreadsheet scope.

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:57.060
Notice that there
are no spread--

00:12:57.060 --> 00:12:59.040
sorry, there are no passwords.

00:12:59.040 --> 00:13:01.710
There are no OAuth keys.

00:13:01.710 --> 00:13:04.740
There are no secrets,
none of that stuff,

00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:07.650
because Tess just
gave access to--

00:13:07.650 --> 00:13:11.850
for the service account
that this code runs as,

00:13:11.850 --> 00:13:14.070
she added that service
account as a collaborator

00:13:14.070 --> 00:13:15.270
on the spreadsheet.

00:13:15.270 --> 00:13:18.420
And boom, all the
access is taken care of.

00:13:18.420 --> 00:13:21.540
After that, we need to
create a client object.

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:24.990
And then here is actually where
the call to Google spreadsheets

00:13:24.990 --> 00:13:26.186
is done.

00:13:26.186 --> 00:13:30.430
And we need to send two
things along as parameters--

00:13:30.430 --> 00:13:33.540
the spreadsheetId, of course--
that's that long ID-looking

00:13:33.540 --> 00:13:35.700
thing that's up in
the address bar--

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:38.490
and then the range, so
what columns and rows

00:13:38.490 --> 00:13:40.410
we want to read.

00:13:40.410 --> 00:13:44.980
Once you have that you have a
response.data.values object.

00:13:44.980 --> 00:13:47.700
And in this one, you have
a two dimensional array

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:51.150
of the cells from
the spreadsheet.

00:13:51.150 --> 00:13:55.200
Put a little set logic
and stuff on top of this,

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:57.630
and you have the
service we just ran.

00:13:57.630 --> 00:14:02.542
And it clocks in at-- how many
lines of code was it in total?

00:14:02.542 --> 00:14:06.050
ADAM ROSS: Less than 100 lines,
especially without the 30 more

00:14:06.050 --> 00:14:09.560
for authenticating to
an API on top of it.

00:14:09.560 --> 00:14:11.000
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yeah, nobody likes

00:14:11.000 --> 00:14:13.560
to write authentication code.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:15.600
So this is a great success.

00:14:15.600 --> 00:14:17.040
Sang, he loves it.

00:14:17.040 --> 00:14:19.050
He says to Tess,
this is amazing.

00:14:19.050 --> 00:14:20.630
I love this approach.

00:14:20.630 --> 00:14:23.000
And Tess, of course, she
wants to build on that success

00:14:23.000 --> 00:14:25.190
and say, yes, yes,
there are many more ways

00:14:25.190 --> 00:14:26.630
we can use microservices.

00:14:26.630 --> 00:14:30.290
We still have this big
monolith over here, remember.

00:14:30.290 --> 00:14:32.090
Now, Tess and
team, they sit down

00:14:32.090 --> 00:14:36.732
and plan out the third iteration
of the recommendation engine.

00:14:36.732 --> 00:14:38.690
They have a few different
things they could do.

00:14:38.690 --> 00:14:41.930
Like, machine learning perhaps
could drive recommendations

00:14:41.930 --> 00:14:43.670
instead of the spreadsheet.

00:14:43.670 --> 00:14:46.760
They could drive it off
of the current customers'

00:14:46.760 --> 00:14:48.350
previous orders.

00:14:48.350 --> 00:14:51.320
They could analyze
orders in aggregate.

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:54.550
There's so many
things they could do.

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:58.640
But before they have
time to build version 3,

00:14:58.640 --> 00:15:01.850
it's time to break out some
logic from the existing

00:15:01.850 --> 00:15:02.840
monolith.

00:15:02.840 --> 00:15:05.670
Because Sang comes to
[? Beth ?] and says, look,

00:15:05.670 --> 00:15:08.330
we are really having a
problem with payments.

00:15:08.330 --> 00:15:09.890
Payments are just not working.

00:15:09.890 --> 00:15:12.470
We're losing revenue
right and left.

00:15:12.470 --> 00:15:17.840
Could you sprinkle some
microservice magic on payments

00:15:17.840 --> 00:15:19.710
and make them work?

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:21.320
What will [? Beth ?] say here?

00:15:21.320 --> 00:15:22.880
Yes, of course.

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:23.840
Let's do it.

00:15:23.840 --> 00:15:26.270
This is a great way of
getting more microservices

00:15:26.270 --> 00:15:29.040
into the company.

00:15:29.040 --> 00:15:33.560
So first off, she looks at the
existing code for payments.

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:36.230
And I don't know,
many of us have

00:15:36.230 --> 00:15:39.620
worked in existing code bases,
and we may have seen something

00:15:39.620 --> 00:15:40.580
like this.

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:42.420
Sprinkled throughout
the monolith

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:44.390
are various payment
operations that

00:15:44.390 --> 00:15:47.580
go off to the external
payment provider.

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:50.390
But also, there are
these weird ways

00:15:50.390 --> 00:15:52.100
where some squiggly
line goes off

00:15:52.100 --> 00:15:55.790
through some weird
libraries that we think

00:15:55.790 --> 00:15:59.030
hits the payment processor.

00:15:59.030 --> 00:16:02.210
There is also this odd thing,
where this piece of code that

00:16:02.210 --> 00:16:04.910
hits the previous
payment processor,

00:16:04.910 --> 00:16:07.790
we don't think it's being
used, but who knows.

00:16:07.790 --> 00:16:10.070
It's checked into
source control.

00:16:10.070 --> 00:16:12.740
There's also this other
line off in a module that's

00:16:12.740 --> 00:16:15.200
not about payments at all that
goes off to somewhere else,

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:17.990
and we're not quite sure where.

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:21.190
This is the way existing
code bases often look, right?

00:16:21.190 --> 00:16:23.450
It's sort of accretes over time.

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:27.030
And nobody really knows
what's going on in all places.

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:29.930
And nobody dares go
in and delete it all.

00:16:29.930 --> 00:16:33.500
So Tess, she thinks, we can
really get some focus here

00:16:33.500 --> 00:16:34.910
with microservices.

00:16:34.910 --> 00:16:36.310
Let's see how that
would be done.

00:16:36.310 --> 00:16:37.880
ADAM ROSS: You know, it
seems like one of the biggest

00:16:37.880 --> 00:16:40.080
challenges is that payment
is really critical,

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:43.682
but there's no one who's
owning all those pieces.

00:16:43.682 --> 00:16:45.140
Well, with a
microservice approach,

00:16:45.140 --> 00:16:48.560
you can at least have one
service in the code base,

00:16:48.560 --> 00:16:50.930
in the solution, that
someone, some team,

00:16:50.930 --> 00:16:54.590
can own and make sure it
doesn't start becoming squiggly.

00:16:54.590 --> 00:16:56.840
So here, we only have
very straight lines,

00:16:56.840 --> 00:17:00.230
showing the API calls from
the monolith making requests

00:17:00.230 --> 00:17:02.570
to the payment service, which
does all the interactions

00:17:02.570 --> 00:17:04.130
with the payment processor.

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:05.793
That also has the
nice result of being

00:17:05.793 --> 00:17:07.460
able to swap out that
payment processor,

00:17:07.460 --> 00:17:12.319
if you want to make a change,
and only touch the one service.

00:17:12.319 --> 00:17:17.089
That API, that needs to be
thought through, though.

00:17:17.089 --> 00:17:21.260
What exactly are those various
operations this payment service

00:17:21.260 --> 00:17:23.750
should support?

00:17:23.750 --> 00:17:26.480
Well, before getting into
a technical design session,

00:17:26.480 --> 00:17:28.160
we need to be strategic.

00:17:28.160 --> 00:17:30.590
A technique called event
storming might be used here

00:17:30.590 --> 00:17:33.170
to figure out what are all
the different kinds of things

00:17:33.170 --> 00:17:35.710
that might belong in
a payment service?

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:38.270
And this requires both
the technical experts

00:17:38.270 --> 00:17:41.570
and the subject matter
experts to get into the room.

00:17:41.570 --> 00:17:45.050
The technical experts
might know specific things

00:17:45.050 --> 00:17:46.640
about the existing
implementation

00:17:46.640 --> 00:17:49.100
and have a lot of insight into
the internal implementation

00:17:49.100 --> 00:17:50.490
events that matter.

00:17:50.490 --> 00:17:53.900
But the domain experts are going
to have a much clearer idea

00:17:53.900 --> 00:17:57.410
of all the things that haven't
been possible to do so far

00:17:57.410 --> 00:17:58.850
or might be really
important to do

00:17:58.850 --> 00:18:04.470
in a year that could be critical
to inform the architecture.

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:06.920
So once you have a
long list of events

00:18:06.920 --> 00:18:08.420
that all seem
related to payments,

00:18:08.420 --> 00:18:11.150
it's time to start separating
them out into two piles.

00:18:11.150 --> 00:18:13.930
What are the events you'll
include in your payment

00:18:13.930 --> 00:18:14.430
service?

00:18:14.430 --> 00:18:15.888
And what are the
events that you're

00:18:15.888 --> 00:18:18.920
going to exclude as being
not quite focused enough

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:20.490
to belong here?

00:18:20.490 --> 00:18:23.930
So you might authorize payments,
charge a previously authorized

00:18:23.930 --> 00:18:27.540
payment, refunds, cancellations.

00:18:27.540 --> 00:18:31.250
But excluded from that, an
inventory function, order

00:18:31.250 --> 00:18:32.780
status and shipping.

00:18:32.780 --> 00:18:36.140
These are things that might be
intimately related to payments

00:18:36.140 --> 00:18:39.260
but are a different domain
area with different owners

00:18:39.260 --> 00:18:40.490
and different problems.

00:18:40.490 --> 00:18:43.640
Maybe those are microservices
for the next exercise.

00:18:46.472 --> 00:18:47.670
MARTIN OMANDER: All right.

00:18:47.670 --> 00:18:52.140
So Sang, the CTO
and Tess's boss,

00:18:52.140 --> 00:18:53.850
he's really encouraged by this.

00:18:53.850 --> 00:18:56.820
Now, we actually know what
we're doing with payments.

00:18:56.820 --> 00:19:02.260
But, he says, there's
one thing missing here.

00:19:02.260 --> 00:19:02.850
There we go.

00:19:02.850 --> 00:19:04.260
There's one thing missing here.

00:19:04.260 --> 00:19:08.520
Today with the monolith, we
are dropping some payments.

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:10.680
Now, you're breaking
out the payment service

00:19:10.680 --> 00:19:12.810
from the monolith,
which is great.

00:19:12.810 --> 00:19:15.780
But we could still
have this problem

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:17.520
that we have with
the monolith today.

00:19:17.520 --> 00:19:21.060
So what if the monolith sends
an authorization request,

00:19:21.060 --> 00:19:24.780
like authorize this credit
card number for $100,

00:19:24.780 --> 00:19:27.660
and somebody put a comma in
the wrong place in the payment

00:19:27.660 --> 00:19:28.680
service?

00:19:28.680 --> 00:19:30.330
Or the network is down?

00:19:30.330 --> 00:19:33.880
Or the payment
processor is down?

00:19:33.880 --> 00:19:36.150
Then a 500 is returned
from the service.

00:19:36.150 --> 00:19:37.020
OK, that's good.

00:19:37.020 --> 00:19:40.953
But we're still in this
position where we've lost money.

00:19:40.953 --> 00:19:42.870
We have a payment that
hasn't been authorized.

00:19:42.870 --> 00:19:45.870
We cannot charge
this payment later.

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:50.920
How does your microservice
magic fix that?

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:54.310
Tess thinks this through,
reads up on it a little bit,

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:56.380
and she comes up
with the solution.

00:19:56.380 --> 00:19:59.200
Asynchronous messaging
is the way to go,

00:19:59.200 --> 00:20:01.840
because instead of just having
the monolith talk directly

00:20:01.840 --> 00:20:05.530
to the payment service, it can
go through Cloud Tasks, which

00:20:05.530 --> 00:20:08.980
is a fairly new product in
the Google Cloud Platform.

00:20:08.980 --> 00:20:11.150
Let's see how that would work.

00:20:11.150 --> 00:20:12.790
So the monolith
would send a message,

00:20:12.790 --> 00:20:16.270
authorize this credit card
for $100 just like before.

00:20:16.270 --> 00:20:19.000
Fire and forget, needs
to do this once only.

00:20:19.000 --> 00:20:21.840
Cloud Tasks would
then send that message

00:20:21.840 --> 00:20:23.740
on to the payment service.

00:20:23.740 --> 00:20:25.210
Somebody put a comma wrong.

00:20:25.210 --> 00:20:26.710
There was a network
error, whatever.

00:20:26.710 --> 00:20:28.330
There's a failure.

00:20:28.330 --> 00:20:30.250
The payment service is
a well-behaved service.

00:20:30.250 --> 00:20:33.380
It returns a 500 server error.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:35.650
Now here, things
are different, start

00:20:35.650 --> 00:20:37.570
going different from
the previous slide.

00:20:37.570 --> 00:20:40.600
The Cloud Tasks component
doesn't give up.

00:20:40.600 --> 00:20:45.400
It will keep resending that
until there is a success.

00:20:45.400 --> 00:20:50.320
And then as soon as the status
code returned from the service

00:20:50.320 --> 00:20:53.650
is in the 200s, that's
how Cloud Tasks knows

00:20:53.650 --> 00:20:55.450
that, OK, I can
lean back, I don't

00:20:55.450 --> 00:20:56.843
have to send it any more times.

00:20:56.843 --> 00:20:58.510
ADAM ROSS: Hey, Martin,
how long will it

00:20:58.510 --> 00:21:00.850
keep trying those requests?

00:21:00.850 --> 00:21:03.040
MARTIN OMANDER:
Yeah, you can set it.

00:21:03.040 --> 00:21:05.680
But at a maximum,
it can be 30 days.

00:21:05.680 --> 00:21:07.990
And you can set it to
shorter if you want to.

00:21:07.990 --> 00:21:10.760
You can also adjust the
exponential backoff behavior

00:21:10.760 --> 00:21:11.260
here.

00:21:13.800 --> 00:21:18.300
OK, so Sang says, good,
good, that will work.

00:21:18.300 --> 00:21:19.980
And you know, I talked
to the ops team.

00:21:19.980 --> 00:21:23.250
They really liked this
microservice approach.

00:21:23.250 --> 00:21:28.230
But, Tess, those Cloud Functions
we had before JavaScript--

00:21:28.230 --> 00:21:31.860
we uploaded them to Google,
we didn't really know where

00:21:31.860 --> 00:21:33.570
and when they were running--

00:21:33.570 --> 00:21:36.330
the ops team, they want to
be a little more hands-on

00:21:36.330 --> 00:21:39.510
and have more control
of the stack here.

00:21:39.510 --> 00:21:44.280
How can we get that with your
microservice approach, Tess?

00:21:44.280 --> 00:21:47.400
Tess thinks about it and
says, well, it actually--

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:49.320
do they want containers?

00:21:49.320 --> 00:21:52.530
And Sang says, yes, containers
is exactly what they want.

00:21:52.530 --> 00:21:56.220
But you know what, containers
is usually a lot of work.

00:21:56.220 --> 00:21:59.220
We want that sort of low
ops you've been telling us

00:21:59.220 --> 00:22:04.780
about that serverless has but
the control of containers.

00:22:04.780 --> 00:22:05.835
How would they do that?

00:22:05.835 --> 00:22:07.335
ADAM ROSS: Yeah,
Tess doesn't really

00:22:07.335 --> 00:22:10.140
have time to stand up a
Kubernetes cluster today.

00:22:10.140 --> 00:22:12.540
Luckily, there's a new
solution for this--

00:22:12.540 --> 00:22:15.480
Cloud Run, a new
beta product that

00:22:15.480 --> 00:22:18.990
marries the worlds of
serverless with containers.

00:22:18.990 --> 00:22:23.400
You can wrap your
service in a Dockerfile,

00:22:23.400 --> 00:22:26.310
build a container image,
and throw it at the cloud.

00:22:26.310 --> 00:22:28.710
And it will answer
HTTP requests.

00:22:28.710 --> 00:22:30.720
And then if this service
were to keep growing

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:33.450
and the Into The
Woods ops team wanted

00:22:33.450 --> 00:22:36.240
to get more heavily
involved, Cloud Run on GKE

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:38.640
would allow them to manage
that Kubernetes cluster

00:22:38.640 --> 00:22:41.932
and move those containers
over as they deploy them

00:22:41.932 --> 00:22:42.765
to the new location.

00:22:46.370 --> 00:22:49.900
So using Cloud Run, we
have all requests coming in

00:22:49.900 --> 00:22:53.380
from the monolith,
hitting Cloud Tasks now

00:22:53.380 --> 00:22:56.320
as a way to ensure the
resiliency of the system.

00:22:56.320 --> 00:23:01.120
And then Cloud Tasks will
pace out the requests

00:23:01.120 --> 00:23:03.760
to Cloud Run, which will then
send them on to the payment

00:23:03.760 --> 00:23:06.310
processor and
stores status events

00:23:06.310 --> 00:23:10.960
in Cloud Firestore, a
unstructured serverless storage

00:23:10.960 --> 00:23:11.460
option.

00:23:11.460 --> 00:23:13.918
MARTIN OMANDER: So what do you
mean when you say serverless

00:23:13.918 --> 00:23:15.280
for the database there, Adam?

00:23:15.280 --> 00:23:16.780
ADAM ROSS: A lot
of database systems

00:23:16.780 --> 00:23:18.030
aren't really serverless.

00:23:18.030 --> 00:23:21.910
And serverless compute systems,
like Cloud Run or Cloud

00:23:21.910 --> 00:23:23.350
Functions, are
going to scale much

00:23:23.350 --> 00:23:29.170
higher than a typical single
instance is able to go.

00:23:29.170 --> 00:23:30.700
So that's where
Firestore comes in.

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:31.450
MARTIN OMANDER: Cool.

00:23:31.450 --> 00:23:33.200
ADAM ROSS: It also has
some nice features,

00:23:33.200 --> 00:23:34.255
like eventing on updates.

00:23:37.690 --> 00:23:41.090
So you might wonder, oh,
what kinds of crazy hijinks

00:23:41.090 --> 00:23:44.125
do I need to get to in my code
to build a Cloud Run service?

00:23:44.125 --> 00:23:45.125
MARTIN OMANDER: [LAUGHS]

00:23:45.125 --> 00:23:49.740
ADAM ROSS: This is a pretty
simple almost "hello world"

00:23:49.740 --> 00:23:52.260
like application
written in Go that

00:23:52.260 --> 00:23:54.000
is going to respond
to any request

00:23:54.000 --> 00:23:55.980
with "Payment Approved,"
which is how we all

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:59.860
would like payment processors
to authorize our credit cards.

00:23:59.860 --> 00:24:02.550
MARTIN OMANDER: So there was
no Cloud Run-specific code

00:24:02.550 --> 00:24:03.630
there that I could see.

00:24:03.630 --> 00:24:06.340
ADAM ROSS: No, there
is absolutely none.

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:10.800
The one wrinkle perhaps is that
the port that your HTTP service

00:24:10.800 --> 00:24:14.760
needs to bind is going to come
in from a port environment

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:16.840
variable, which is
a little unusual.

00:24:16.840 --> 00:24:19.560
But it's still
flexible, and you can

00:24:19.560 --> 00:24:22.740
override that as you need to.

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:25.220
So this service needs to
be wrapped in a Dockerfile.

00:24:25.220 --> 00:24:27.920
A Dockerfile is a relatively
straightforward package

00:24:27.920 --> 00:24:31.550
manifest using industry
standards around how containers

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:35.270
work, be it GKE or Cloud Run.

00:24:35.270 --> 00:24:38.720
And this has two stages,
a little more complicated

00:24:38.720 --> 00:24:39.920
than most Dockerfiles.

00:24:39.920 --> 00:24:41.600
But this has a
build stage, which

00:24:41.600 --> 00:24:44.930
is going to compile our
Go code into a binary;

00:24:44.930 --> 00:24:47.240
and then a production
stage, which

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:51.800
pulls from a stripped down
variant of Linux called Alpine,

00:24:51.800 --> 00:24:54.500
copy in that binary,
and then run it whenever

00:24:54.500 --> 00:24:55.940
this container is started up.

00:24:59.350 --> 00:25:01.960
Deploying is two
commands here, one

00:25:01.960 --> 00:25:06.460
using Cloud Build to take all
of this service and Dockerfile,

00:25:06.460 --> 00:25:09.850
upload it to Cloud Build to
have a container image prepared

00:25:09.850 --> 00:25:14.260
and sent on to Google
Container Registry.

00:25:14.260 --> 00:25:19.510
Then to deploy, gcloud beta run
deploy takes that container,

00:25:19.510 --> 00:25:22.930
puts it into Cloud Run,
and starts you serving.

00:25:22.930 --> 00:25:25.750
I have found that the first
time might be a little bit slow,

00:25:25.750 --> 00:25:28.130
but most deployments
take about 30 seconds.

00:25:34.860 --> 00:25:37.070
So that service, ready to go.

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:39.470
Let's start rolling
out into production.

00:25:39.470 --> 00:25:41.870
Now, as a whole new
service and doing a mission

00:25:41.870 --> 00:25:43.550
critical function,
you might not want

00:25:43.550 --> 00:25:46.620
to have it immediately take
over all payment processing.

00:25:46.620 --> 00:25:50.120
So the monolith is going to
need to be slightly adjusted

00:25:50.120 --> 00:25:54.230
to send just some of the
traffic to this new service.

00:25:54.230 --> 00:25:57.770
That means, unfortunately, going
into all the gnarly old code

00:25:57.770 --> 00:25:59.990
and making adjustments there.

00:25:59.990 --> 00:26:02.102
Hopefully, you've already
got an audit in place

00:26:02.102 --> 00:26:04.310
to figure out where all that
is so it can be deleted.

00:26:08.864 --> 00:26:11.460
MARTIN OMANDER: So they deploy,
they do this canary release.

00:26:11.460 --> 00:26:14.670
They send 1% of the traffic
through the new payment

00:26:14.670 --> 00:26:15.450
service.

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:17.220
It works great.

00:26:17.220 --> 00:26:21.690
They send 5% through
the new payment service.

00:26:21.690 --> 00:26:22.530
It works great.

00:26:22.530 --> 00:26:25.800
10%, they-- eventually, 50%.

00:26:25.800 --> 00:26:29.130
They send 100% through,
and it's working.

00:26:29.130 --> 00:26:30.630
No payments are dropped.

00:26:30.630 --> 00:26:32.490
Everything scales beautifully.

00:26:32.490 --> 00:26:34.800
The ops team is happy,
because they have containers

00:26:34.800 --> 00:26:37.350
and the magic of serverless.

00:26:37.350 --> 00:26:40.560
This seemed to be a
very easy rollout.

00:26:40.560 --> 00:26:43.140
But we all know that
there is no such thing

00:26:43.140 --> 00:26:44.590
as a trouble-free rollout.

00:26:44.590 --> 00:26:47.070
There is always something
that doesn't go exactly

00:26:47.070 --> 00:26:49.050
like you expected.

00:26:49.050 --> 00:26:51.180
In this case, Thalia,
the controller--

00:26:51.180 --> 00:26:53.460
we've seen her before-- she
comes into Tess's office

00:26:53.460 --> 00:26:57.610
and says, hey, all my
financial reports are broken.

00:26:57.610 --> 00:26:59.220
This was a week
after they ramped up.

00:26:59.220 --> 00:27:01.770
On Friday, she comes in,
all my reports are broken.

00:27:01.770 --> 00:27:03.210
Why is that?

00:27:03.210 --> 00:27:05.790
Tess starts looking
at this closer

00:27:05.790 --> 00:27:07.860
and sees that, ah,
one of the reports

00:27:07.860 --> 00:27:10.740
actually has two pieces of data.

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:13.260
One piece lives in the monolith.

00:27:13.260 --> 00:27:17.380
And the other piece now
lives in the payment service.

00:27:17.380 --> 00:27:20.280
And before, when everything
lived in the monolith,

00:27:20.280 --> 00:27:21.990
it was far easier to
run these reports,

00:27:21.990 --> 00:27:25.860
because both pieces of
data were in the monolith.

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:27.060
How do we fix this?

00:27:27.060 --> 00:27:28.890
This, by the way, if
you've been working

00:27:28.890 --> 00:27:30.960
on migrating to
microservices, you

00:27:30.960 --> 00:27:34.140
will have seen this problem
in one form or another.

00:27:34.140 --> 00:27:37.980
Or if you haven't
yet, you soon will.

00:27:37.980 --> 00:27:40.350
This is data fragmentation--

00:27:40.350 --> 00:27:43.040
big problem, especially
for reporting.

00:27:43.040 --> 00:27:45.540
So of course, the problem here
is we have the data over here

00:27:45.540 --> 00:27:47.220
on the far left,
and on the right

00:27:47.220 --> 00:27:50.790
is Thalia, who wants
her report, right?

00:27:50.790 --> 00:27:52.530
Well, it turns out--

00:27:52.530 --> 00:27:54.390
Tess looks into this,
and it turns out

00:27:54.390 --> 00:27:57.840
that Cloud Firestore
has triggers.

00:27:57.840 --> 00:28:02.310
So whenever something's
written, she

00:28:02.310 --> 00:28:03.780
can trigger, build
a new service,

00:28:03.780 --> 00:28:06.130
an ETL service,
that is triggered.

00:28:06.130 --> 00:28:07.920
It can sanitize the data.

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:10.500
It can do some light
data transformation

00:28:10.500 --> 00:28:16.860
and then put the data in a
data lake, or reporting service

00:28:16.860 --> 00:28:18.390
if you wish.

00:28:18.390 --> 00:28:22.470
The monolith has an
existing reporting module.

00:28:22.470 --> 00:28:26.880
That one needs to be
repointed to this data lake,

00:28:26.880 --> 00:28:31.740
so it sends its transactions
and its orders to the data lake.

00:28:31.740 --> 00:28:35.430
Now, all the data is in the data
lake, the reporting service.

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:38.090
This is kind of like
in the monolith days

00:28:38.090 --> 00:28:40.800
they probably ran a
separate reporting server.

00:28:40.800 --> 00:28:44.580
This is the equivalent
in serverless land,

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:46.260
microservice land.

00:28:46.260 --> 00:28:48.360
Now that all the data
sits in the data lake,

00:28:48.360 --> 00:28:52.530
they can now build the
reports off of that.

00:28:52.530 --> 00:28:55.860
So Thalia had to go without
her reports for a few days.

00:28:55.860 --> 00:28:57.680
It was not ideal.

00:28:57.680 --> 00:28:59.730
They had forgotten
about reports when

00:28:59.730 --> 00:29:01.620
they did the event storming.

00:29:01.620 --> 00:29:04.970
But they didn't lose any money.

00:29:04.970 --> 00:29:07.800
Black Friday rolls around.

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:10.800
Now, we're running
into a big problem.

00:29:10.800 --> 00:29:14.370
Sang goes into Tess's office
and say, all the API calls

00:29:14.370 --> 00:29:17.330
to the payment processor are
returning "quota exceeded"

00:29:17.330 --> 00:29:17.830
errors.

00:29:17.830 --> 00:29:19.320
What should we do?

00:29:19.320 --> 00:29:21.820
All hands on deck.

00:29:21.820 --> 00:29:22.860
What is going on?

00:29:22.860 --> 00:29:26.040
This is something that if you're
migrating to microservices,

00:29:26.040 --> 00:29:29.220
if you haven't run into
this sort of problem before,

00:29:29.220 --> 00:29:31.140
you soon will.

00:29:31.140 --> 00:29:34.080
The problem here, of course, is
you have a chain of components

00:29:34.080 --> 00:29:35.280
talking to each other.

00:29:35.280 --> 00:29:39.240
And they all have different
scaling characteristics.

00:29:39.240 --> 00:29:43.590
So some of them scale very
well, and others not so well.

00:29:43.590 --> 00:29:47.370
For example, they may
have a contractual rate

00:29:47.370 --> 00:29:49.530
limit with a payment processor.

00:29:49.530 --> 00:29:51.540
You can only send
one transaction

00:29:51.540 --> 00:29:53.430
a second or something like that.

00:29:53.430 --> 00:29:56.610
You might also have seen it if
the component on the far right

00:29:56.610 --> 00:29:59.490
is a SQL database that's
not serverless and doesn't

00:29:59.490 --> 00:30:00.745
scale as well.

00:30:00.745 --> 00:30:02.370
You might have seen
it if you connected

00:30:02.370 --> 00:30:06.960
to an HR system or a CMS
system or other system that

00:30:06.960 --> 00:30:11.490
can't handle the kind of load
that serverless can handle.

00:30:11.490 --> 00:30:13.950
What do we do?

00:30:13.950 --> 00:30:16.060
If you read and check
out the literature,

00:30:16.060 --> 00:30:19.860
you will see lots of
thoughts on how to fix this.

00:30:19.860 --> 00:30:21.660
Like, you can
write all this code

00:30:21.660 --> 00:30:24.390
to write a circuit
breaker component.

00:30:24.390 --> 00:30:26.540
Or you can implement
back pressure

00:30:26.540 --> 00:30:28.290
that leads back through,
that ripples back

00:30:28.290 --> 00:30:29.082
through this chain.

00:30:29.082 --> 00:30:31.350
There are so many
things you can do.

00:30:31.350 --> 00:30:34.170
Most of them involve
a lot of coding.

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:36.540
And we all know, if you
write a lot of new code

00:30:36.540 --> 00:30:39.360
to fix something,
you might fix it,

00:30:39.360 --> 00:30:43.170
you will also introduce
a lot of new bugs.

00:30:43.170 --> 00:30:48.360
Then Tess remembers,
they picked Cloud Tasks.

00:30:48.360 --> 00:30:51.510
Cloud Tasks actually has
this knob you can turn.

00:30:51.510 --> 00:30:53.280
You can adjust the rate.

00:30:53.280 --> 00:30:56.100
So the monolith can throw
any amount of transactions

00:30:56.100 --> 00:30:57.690
per second at Tasks.

00:30:57.690 --> 00:30:59.670
Tasks will absorb it.

00:30:59.670 --> 00:31:04.950
And then it will only send
out to Cloud Run as many

00:31:04.950 --> 00:31:06.600
as it's asked to.

00:31:06.600 --> 00:31:10.740
So [? Beth ?] goes in and
checks the Cloud Tasks console.

00:31:10.740 --> 00:31:12.600
And she sees that,
oh my gosh, we have

00:31:12.600 --> 00:31:14.520
a lot of backed up tasks here.

00:31:14.520 --> 00:31:18.150
2,500 transactions are
sitting there and waiting

00:31:18.150 --> 00:31:21.240
to be authorized or charged.

00:31:21.240 --> 00:31:22.770
Because they're
using Cloud Tasks,

00:31:22.770 --> 00:31:26.970
they can go in and change,
turn that knob and set

00:31:26.970 --> 00:31:31.350
max-tasks-dispatched-per-second
to 1.

00:31:31.350 --> 00:31:32.620
The default is 5.

00:31:32.620 --> 00:31:34.540
They turn it down to 1.

00:31:34.540 --> 00:31:37.140
Now, all of sudden, they're
only sending 1 per second

00:31:37.140 --> 00:31:38.340
to the payment provider.

00:31:38.340 --> 00:31:41.160
The payment provider
accepts the traffic.

00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:43.950
And after about
45 minutes or so,

00:31:43.950 --> 00:31:48.060
they will have worked
through this backlog.

00:31:48.060 --> 00:31:49.620
Excellent.

00:31:49.620 --> 00:31:53.910
Tess is still a little
shaky, shooken up over this.

00:31:53.910 --> 00:31:56.220
But Sang says, this is
actually a great job.

00:31:56.220 --> 00:31:59.460
Any major transformation,
any major migration

00:31:59.460 --> 00:32:02.260
will have some
hiccups along the way.

00:32:02.260 --> 00:32:05.250
This microservice approach
lets us move faster

00:32:05.250 --> 00:32:06.480
and experiment more.

00:32:06.480 --> 00:32:08.650
He loves it.

00:32:08.650 --> 00:32:12.000
Tess, for her part,
she's just very happy

00:32:12.000 --> 00:32:15.164
that this worked out.

00:32:15.164 --> 00:32:17.860
ADAM ROSS: Well, now that Tess
has been undoubtedly promoted

00:32:17.860 --> 00:32:22.630
to chief microservices scientist
of Into The Woods, it's time

00:32:22.630 --> 00:32:25.210
to start thinking about a
more deliberative approach

00:32:25.210 --> 00:32:28.370
to how they do
microservices in the future.

00:32:28.370 --> 00:32:30.820
So one lesson that she might
have learned from all of this

00:32:30.820 --> 00:32:33.340
is that a microservice
is a good fit

00:32:33.340 --> 00:32:35.710
for well-understood problems.

00:32:35.710 --> 00:32:38.380
A user profile service
might be easy for anyone

00:32:38.380 --> 00:32:40.480
to understand and
anticipate in advance,

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:42.800
so maybe that can be created.

00:32:42.800 --> 00:32:45.340
But for harder
challenges, for areas

00:32:45.340 --> 00:32:48.940
that need a lot more innovation
and exploratory development,

00:32:48.940 --> 00:32:54.220
trying to stay on the
path of a microservice

00:32:54.220 --> 00:32:55.840
might be a source
of slowdowns, might

00:32:55.840 --> 00:32:57.850
get in the way a little bit.

00:32:57.850 --> 00:32:59.560
So it's important
not to always be

00:32:59.560 --> 00:33:02.890
too focused on building a
microservice on the things

00:33:02.890 --> 00:33:07.470
you don't yet have a mastery of.

00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:10.050
Moving from there,
Sang and Tess,

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:13.608
they might have talked
about how many microservices

00:33:13.608 --> 00:33:14.400
do we want to have?

00:33:14.400 --> 00:33:18.240
Should we have 1,000 by the time
we're done with all of this?

00:33:18.240 --> 00:33:22.890
Well, feel that out as well,
move a little bit more slowly.

00:33:22.890 --> 00:33:26.970
You see, the trade-off
of microservices

00:33:26.970 --> 00:33:31.110
is really between developers and
operators, or least development

00:33:31.110 --> 00:33:32.730
and operations.

00:33:32.730 --> 00:33:34.740
When you have a
small service, it

00:33:34.740 --> 00:33:37.770
is much easier for a
developer to really get

00:33:37.770 --> 00:33:41.200
into all of the code and
develop an expertise in it.

00:33:41.200 --> 00:33:43.500
And so when you have
more small services,

00:33:43.500 --> 00:33:46.030
development complexity
might go down.

00:33:46.030 --> 00:33:50.490
But as you add more and more
services and more interactions

00:33:50.490 --> 00:33:54.030
between them, you start to gain
more operational complexity--

00:33:54.030 --> 00:33:57.720
more deployments, more
cascade failure possibilities,

00:33:57.720 --> 00:33:59.590
and harder troubleshooting.

00:33:59.590 --> 00:34:01.770
So there's a sweet
spot in there.

00:34:01.770 --> 00:34:03.420
That trade-off is
going to be very

00:34:03.420 --> 00:34:06.030
unique to every
organization, because it's

00:34:06.030 --> 00:34:07.530
very particular to the people.

00:34:07.530 --> 00:34:10.230
Do you have more senior
operations engineers or more

00:34:10.230 --> 00:34:12.239
senior application developers?

00:34:12.239 --> 00:34:14.969
Between those two, you'll
find your sweet spot.

00:34:14.969 --> 00:34:17.489
Now, with a serverless
approach where

00:34:17.489 --> 00:34:19.920
you've taken a lot
of that ops overhead

00:34:19.920 --> 00:34:25.650
and pushed it onto the cloud,
that line for ops complexity

00:34:25.650 --> 00:34:27.989
will flatten a little bit.

00:34:27.989 --> 00:34:30.210
And that means your sweet
spot can slide over.

00:34:30.210 --> 00:34:32.100
And it becomes more
reasonable to have

00:34:32.100 --> 00:34:34.889
a larger number of
microservices in your arsenal.

00:34:41.770 --> 00:34:45.699
So as they've been
adding new microservices

00:34:45.699 --> 00:34:48.159
and splitting some
off from the monolith,

00:34:48.159 --> 00:34:51.370
each of those services
has a stakeholder.

00:34:51.370 --> 00:34:54.130
This is an owner, whether
it's on the business side

00:34:54.130 --> 00:34:57.370
or otherwise, that
understands that service

00:34:57.370 --> 00:34:59.440
and the role of that
technology in the company.

00:34:59.440 --> 00:35:02.080
They can be a champion
of that technology

00:35:02.080 --> 00:35:04.570
through the rest of the
company, but they can also

00:35:04.570 --> 00:35:08.560
be an ongoing subject matter
expert for the service.

00:35:08.560 --> 00:35:10.570
This kind of direct
line of ownership

00:35:10.570 --> 00:35:14.020
also prevents a lot of
confusion about what priorities

00:35:14.020 --> 00:35:15.470
are for an individual service.

00:35:19.230 --> 00:35:21.820
MARTIN OMANDER: So
what did we learn here?

00:35:21.820 --> 00:35:24.620
What are the lessons?

00:35:24.620 --> 00:35:27.850
Tess sits down and thinks
carefully about this.

00:35:27.850 --> 00:35:30.430
This is lessons
that we hope you can

00:35:30.430 --> 00:35:34.240
apply in your organizations.

00:35:34.240 --> 00:35:36.610
One is Conway's law.

00:35:36.610 --> 00:35:41.230
This was created
by Melvin Conway.

00:35:41.230 --> 00:35:43.690
He wrote it down
over 50 years ago.

00:35:43.690 --> 00:35:47.950
It is as true today as when
it was first written down.

00:35:47.950 --> 00:35:50.530
Any of you who has
worked on systems,

00:35:50.530 --> 00:35:53.540
or if you worked on a
website for an organization,

00:35:53.540 --> 00:35:55.660
you've experienced this.

00:35:55.660 --> 00:35:57.880
What this law is
saying is that, if you

00:35:57.880 --> 00:36:01.480
have a certain type of
organizational structure,

00:36:01.480 --> 00:36:04.300
that organizational structure
will lead to-- any system

00:36:04.300 --> 00:36:07.960
that you built will be a mirror
image of that organization.

00:36:07.960 --> 00:36:11.020
You've seen this when building
websites for companies.

00:36:11.020 --> 00:36:13.540
The website hierarchy,
page hierarchy

00:36:13.540 --> 00:36:17.630
tends to follow the org chart.

00:36:17.630 --> 00:36:21.730
So if you have many
small teams, you

00:36:21.730 --> 00:36:25.630
will get many small
microservices.

00:36:25.630 --> 00:36:28.360
That means that you can
actually do an inverse Conway

00:36:28.360 --> 00:36:29.650
maneuver here.

00:36:29.650 --> 00:36:33.880
If you want small services
over there on the right,

00:36:33.880 --> 00:36:36.370
then you create
many small teams.

00:36:36.370 --> 00:36:40.200
If you want a big monolith,
you have just one big room

00:36:40.200 --> 00:36:41.230
full of developers.

00:36:41.230 --> 00:36:45.200
And everybody's
on the same team.

00:36:45.200 --> 00:36:48.360
Next observation is that
weightlifting is really hard

00:36:48.360 --> 00:36:50.180
if you do it by yourself.

00:36:50.180 --> 00:36:52.550
But weightlifting
becomes a lot easier

00:36:52.550 --> 00:36:56.070
if you have like five or six
of your friends to help you.

00:36:56.070 --> 00:36:59.270
So the monolith is really
doing a heavy lift here before,

00:36:59.270 --> 00:37:02.840
but it's far easier when the
other services are helping out.

00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:05.120
As a matter of
fact, the monolith

00:37:05.120 --> 00:37:07.760
doesn't have to do
as much work here,

00:37:07.760 --> 00:37:10.040
because the other
services help out.

00:37:10.040 --> 00:37:12.872
You might even discover that the
monolith is home sick one day

00:37:12.872 --> 00:37:14.330
and can't come in
and lift weights.

00:37:14.330 --> 00:37:18.020
And the other services
can sort of do the lift.

00:37:18.020 --> 00:37:21.570
And eventually, you
might have only services.

00:37:21.570 --> 00:37:24.110
That's far into the
future for Into The Woods,

00:37:24.110 --> 00:37:26.690
but it's something to
consider and think about.

00:37:26.690 --> 00:37:29.720
It's also something that we
need to be a little careful when

00:37:29.720 --> 00:37:33.380
we talk to the
monolith team about.

00:37:33.380 --> 00:37:38.190
We don't talk about how we're
going to shut down their stuff.

00:37:38.190 --> 00:37:41.032
As a matter of fact, this
also has another name

00:37:41.032 --> 00:37:42.240
that you might have heard of.

00:37:42.240 --> 00:37:44.370
That was a little too
confrontational for a slide,

00:37:44.370 --> 00:37:49.990
we felt. Martin Fowler calls
this the strangler pattern.

00:37:49.990 --> 00:37:51.870
This is where you have
a tree in the forest.

00:37:51.870 --> 00:37:53.412
And all these vines
grow up around it

00:37:53.412 --> 00:37:55.245
and suck energy from
the tree and eventually

00:37:55.245 --> 00:37:56.070
killing the tree.

00:37:56.070 --> 00:37:57.940
And then all the
vines are there.

00:37:57.940 --> 00:37:59.545
But team weightlifting--

00:37:59.545 --> 00:38:00.530
[LAUGHTER]

00:38:00.530 --> 00:38:03.750
Let's talk through that instead.

00:38:03.750 --> 00:38:05.580
Then you need an intrapreneur.

00:38:05.580 --> 00:38:10.530
So an intrapreneur is somebody
who has an entrepreneur mindset

00:38:10.530 --> 00:38:13.120
but works inside
an organization.

00:38:13.120 --> 00:38:14.880
This was Tess in this case.

00:38:14.880 --> 00:38:17.500
You need that intrapreneur.

00:38:17.500 --> 00:38:19.950
You also need that opportunity.

00:38:19.950 --> 00:38:23.860
In this case, it was the
recommendation engine.

00:38:23.860 --> 00:38:28.150
And that set them on the
path to microservices.

00:38:28.150 --> 00:38:35.650
So you can be the intrapreneur
in your organization.

00:38:35.650 --> 00:38:38.510
So keep looking for
those opportunities.

00:38:38.510 --> 00:38:40.960
[MUSIC PLAYING]

